# AgnosticEngine — Law of Derivation

## The Law

The `pages` map (`src/data/mock-data.ts`, and later the real DB/API) is the **only** source of truth for both page content and sidebar navigation.

**Corollaries — non-negotiable:**

1. **Presence implies navigability.** A page entry with a `nav` property automatically appears in the sidebar. Removing `nav` makes it unlisted (still reachable by URL). No other step required.

2. **Sub-menus are declared on the child, not the parent.** A page with `nav.parent` set to another page's slug is automatically nested under that parent in the sidebar. The parent page schema is not touched.

3. **Never declare sidebar items separately from pages.** There is no `sidebar.items` array. The sidebar derives itself entirely from the `pages` map. Duplication between pages and nav is a violation of this law.

4. **`sidebar.extras` is the escape hatch — not the pattern.** External links, section dividers, and any non-page nav items go in `layout.sidebar.extras`. This is the only exception and must remain exceptional.

---

## Schema contract

```typescript
// Adding a page to the sidebar = adding nav to the page entry
{
  "/my-page": {
    title: "My Page",
    nav: { label: "My Page", order: 2 },   // ← this is all it takes
    components: [...],
  }
}

// Adding a sub-menu item = adding parent to the child's nav
{
  "/my-page/sub": {
    title: "Sub",
    nav: { label: "Sub", order: 0, parent: "/my-page" },  // ← nested automatically
    components: [...],
  }
}
```

### `nav.order` is a data sort key — NOT CSS `order`
Items are sorted in JavaScript before being written to the DOM. DOM order = visual order = tab order = screen reader order. No accessibility concern.

---

## Sidebar derivation algorithm

```
pages map
  → filter: nav !== undefined
  → split: roots (no parent) vs children (have parent)
  → attach children to parent, sort each group by nav.order
  → merge roots with sidebar.extras (sorted by order)
  → render <nav> in final order
```

The Sidebar (`src/components/organisms/Sidebar.tsx`) implements this. It receives a `NavManifest` (pages without component arrays) and `SidebarExtraItem[]`. No business logic lives inside.

---

## Violations to reject in code review

- A nav link declared anywhere other than `pages[slug].nav`
- A page slug hardcoded inside `Sidebar.tsx` or any layout component
- `sidebar.extras` used for pages that exist in the `pages` map
- `nav.order` applied as a CSS property (use it only as a JS sort key)
- Parent page modified when adding a child page
